+===== arc feedback --help =====
+File or list tool and workflow feedback reports for a registered project.
+
+Resolves `<project>` through the workspace registry from any directory, including outside any Git repository. Unknown projects refuse and list registered project names. Uses the `feedback` journal kind (private by construction, carried by journal exchange) with the default `feedback` scaffold unless `--no-scaffold` is given.
+
+With `--key <slug>`, an open feedback report matching that key receives an appended evidence block instead of creating a duplicate. `--list` prints open feedback keys, routes, and evidence counts for `<project>` without writing. `--route` classifies where the finding belongs (`local`, `upstream`, `operator`, or `delegate`; default `local` on creation). Triage with `arc journal transition` or `arc journal consume --outcome discarded`.
+
+Usage: arc feedback [OPTIONS] <PROJECT>
+
+Arguments:
+  <PROJECT>
+          Registered project name (label or slug) from the workspace registry
+
+Options:
+      --actor <ACTOR>
+          Acting identity; see `arc guide identity-options`
+
+      --body-file <BODY_FILE>
+          Body source: a file path, or '-' for stdin
+
+      --harness <HARNESS>
+          Acting harness; see `arc guide identity-options`
+          
+          [env: ARC_HARNESS]
+
+      --key <SLUG>
+          Cross-session dedup key (kebab-case slug). If an open feedback report with this key exists in the target project's journal, append an evidence block to that report instead of creating a duplicate
+
+      --session <SESSION>
+          Native acting session; see `arc guide identity-options`
+          
+          [env: ARC_SESSION]
+
+      --title <TITLE>
+          Heading for a new feedback report, or title for the appended evidence block when `--key` matches an open report
+
+      --route <ROUTE>
+          Routing classification based on cause: local (Arc-managed tool journal, default on creation), upstream (external issue or PR draft), operator (operator attention or policy decision), or delegate (delegatable follow-up). Omitted when appending via `--key`, preserves the existing report's route
+
+          Possible values:
+          - local:    Local Arc-managed tool or repository journal
+          - upstream: External or upstream tool issue or PR draft
+          - operator: Operator attention or policy decision
+          - delegate: Actionable follow-up suitable for delegation
+
+      --session-link <SESSION_LINK>
+          Private acting-session link; see `arc guide identity-options`
+          
+          [env: ARC_SESSION_LINK]
+
+      --model <MODEL>
+          Declared model and effort; see `arc guide identity-options`
+
+      --scaffold <SCAFFOLD>
+          Scaffold template around the body (.arc/templates/<name>.md or a built-in; defaults to `feedback` on creation)
+
+      --no-scaffold
+          Record the body alone, without the `feedback` default scaffold
+
+      --on-behalf-of <ON_BEHALF_OF>
+          Represented subject; an empty declared model or effort refuses; see `arc guide identity-options`
+          
+          [env: ARC_ON_BEHALF_OF]
+
+      --role <ROLE>
+          Execution boundary; see `arc guide identity-options`
+          
+          [env: ARC_ROLE]
+
+      --source <SPEC>
+          What this item was distilled from, as space-separated `key=value` pairs naming either a recorded session or an intake item. A session needs `harness=<name> session=<id> ts=<rfc3339>`, with `turn=<n>`, `schema=<name>`, and `coverage=<state>` recorded only when supplied. An intake item is exactly `intake=<name> item=<id>`: a name matching `[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}` and one token of at most 128 bytes. The two forms never mix. No transcript text or item body enters the journal
+
+      --change <change_flag>
